{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat reader-brief-claims.md && cat packet-claims-3.json'","aggregated_output":"# Gate 2 claims reading (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Read only this brief and `packet-claims.json`. Do not open other files or run commands or model calls.\n\nEach entry is one answer of a research step to one article. It lists the **claims** the answer must keep, **stored reference items**\n(how an earlier answer kept them), the **link rule**, the **open theses** the answer could link to, the answer's **new theses**, and its\n**kept evidence** items with the thesis ids each links to.\n\nFor each claim, decide whether the answer keeps it: a kept evidence item states the same figure or event for the same measure as the\nclaim (the item may carry more, and the facts may sit in one item). A detail of the claim (a speaker, a count of companies, a\npercentage) is required only where the stored reference items state it. Then decide whether it is linked as the link rule says.\nJudge by meaning, not by matching words. If `answer_usable` is false, every claim is not kept.\n\nReturn one JSON object per entry, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else:\n`{\"n\": 1, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"kept\": true|false, \"linked_as_required\": true|false, \"item\": \"<evidence id or null>\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}]}`\n[\n {\n  \"n\": 61,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per Morgan Stanley's analysis.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from roughly $7.8 million, indicating procurement structure can move final per-rack pricing materially.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 62,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture (the NVL144 cabinet designed to house its Vera Rubin Ultra chips) begin shipping by the end of 2027, its originally slated debut, or slip to 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, versus slipping into 2028 as reported.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of 2027, the period the item says Kyber was originally slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra (the item reports a slip to 2028).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra chips and originally slated to debut in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules within the system remains difficult to manufacture. The report was published in a post on Monday [relative to the July 5-6, 2026 article].\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan — bolting two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power — was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack architecture was delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are due to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 63,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rises from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing procurement structure materially moves the total.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 64,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\",\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from their planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to the article's rendering of Nvidia's claim.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 65,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking. The estimate is cited by Allen Harris of The Berkshire Edge as the capital-spending backdrop supporting the AI rally; it is a broader total-AI-infrastructure measure and does not give a specific 2027 figure for the big four hyperscalers.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In a comparison of Nvidia and Micron, The Berkshire Edge (Allen Harris) cites Nvidia's figures for its latest reported period: revenue growth of about 85 percent, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9 percent and an operating margin of roughly 66 percent, versus Micron's 346 percent revenue growth, 84.6 percent gross margin and 80.4 percent operating margin. The item notes Nvidia still earns more dollars. No reporting period is stated for Nvidia's figures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, a supplier of memory/HBM used alongside AI accelerators, reported for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346 percent growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a gross margin of 84.6 percent (up from 37.7 percent) and an operating margin of 80.4 percent (up from 23.3 percent). The item attributes the boom partly to Nvidia's AI success spilling over into memory demand.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent. That is actual money hitting the income statement now.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs — a concrete signal of memory/storage cost inflation that bears on input costs for memory-dependent hardware including AI accelerators.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 66,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, as reported by fool.com.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, as reported by fool.com.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 67,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, as reported by MarketScale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis cited by MarketScale, NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and MarketScale's key-facts summary states that procurement structure can move final rack pricing by $1+ million per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\",\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 68,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, i.e. the system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis shows memory's share of the Vera Rubin rack rose to 25-30% from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley breakdown reported by the article, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure materially moves the price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 69,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's latest reported quarter produced revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, according to figures cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge comparing Nvidia with Micron. The item does not specify which fiscal quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron's latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia's 85 percent. Micron's GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia's 74.9 percent. Micron's operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia's roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, a supplier of AI memory including high-bandwidth memory used alongside AI accelerators, reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth); GAAP net income was $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin was 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin was 80.4% versus 23.3%, per Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron's gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The action is a pass-through of rising memory costs, a component input also relevant to AI hardware.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, according to Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\",\n     \"That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 70,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank adds that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, moving final pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\",\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported by MarketScale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase from the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory rises to 25-30% of the rack bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200 while the GPU's share falls from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 71,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\",\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers, which plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year), will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capex in 2027 (next year), as reported by fool.com on 7 July 2026.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 72,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com contributor Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, and that this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs. The article gives no company-level capex breakdown and attributes no source for the aggregates.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com contributor Keithen Drury wrote that memory (NAND and DRAM) supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as a result, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices higher still. DRAM is described as the high-speed memory NVIDIA's GPUs use.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in reporting its most recent quarter, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to fool.com's Keithen Drury.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which fool.com's Keithen Drury said is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate; the article states no figure for NVIDIA's expected revenue.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 73,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion the group plans to spend this year (2026), according to the fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 74,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports NVIDIA's latest reported-quarter financials alongside Micron's: year-on-year revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that NVIDIA still earns more absolute dollars. The article does not specify which fiscal quarter these figures cover.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported third-fiscal-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; gross margin of 84.6%, up from 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4%, up from 23.3%. The article attributes the surge to AI-driven memory demand spilling over from NVIDIA's success.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\",\n     \"That is why Nvidia’s success is spilling over into Micron.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 75,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports NVIDIA's latest-quarter financial results in a comparison with Micron: NVIDIA's revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin was 74.9%, and its operating margin was roughly 66%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron's latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia's 85 percent.\",\n     \"Micron's GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia's 74.9 percent.\",\n     \"Micron's operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia's roughly 66 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other plumbing.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\",\n     \"That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth.\",\n     \"GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago.\",\n     \"Micron's gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 76,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"In an article dated 7 July 2026, fool.com author Keithen Drury projects that the AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures will be $650 billion in 2026 and could exceed $1 trillion in 2027, adding that this will drive even higher demand for Nvidia GPUs (and memory chips).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said (reported as recent news) that tight market conditions could last until 2028. The article notes memory supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, memory chip prices have skyrocketed, and new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least another year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher. Nvidia's GPUs use DRAM, so these memory-market conditions bear on Nvidia's input costs and supply.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided next-quarter revenue to about $50 billion, which the article says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying roughly twice that figure in expected Nvidia quarterly revenue. As a memory supplier, Micron's revenue guidance reflects strong memory demand and pricing, an input-cost signal for Nvidia's GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 77,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra generation — begin shipping on its original 2027 schedule, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis claims?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments/volume deployment of the Kyber NVL144 (and the larger NVL576) Rubin Ultra rack systems in calendar 2027 versus a delay to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2027 — set by the item: Kyber 'had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra ... in 2027', and SemiAnalysis says it is delayed by more than 12 months to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because its multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to obtain similar scale-up capacity for Rubin Ultra — has been scrapped, cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back over its odd design and heavy operational burden.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 will run 20% above Wall Street consensus.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 78,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra — begin shipping on its original 2027 timetable, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The first-shipment/availability date of Kyber NVL144 and the Rubin Ultra rack-scale system it houses: 2027 as originally slated, or 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports.\",\n    \"period\": \"End-of-2027 check date: whether Kyber NVL144 has begun shipping by then, measured against the reported >12-month slip to a 2028 debut (both dates stated in the item).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e-kyber-delay\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post on Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, the server cabinet slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules inside the system remains difficult to manufacture. CNBC framed the report as one of a string of reported setbacks raising questions about NVIDIA's product roadmap.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e-nvl576-delay\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, NVIDIA's larger rack system that links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e-backup-cancelled\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's operational burden; the firm said this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra and predicted it could give AMD and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e-nvda-denial\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that the Kyber rack architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e-rubin-shipping\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e-dc-revenue-estimate\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 79,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said (announced 'recently') that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, following a quarter it described with huge growth and strong forward expectations. The item reports memory chip prices have skyrocketed on the demand/supply imbalance and that new industry memory capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n     \"As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article projects that AI hyperscalers plan to spend about $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, which the author says would drive higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 80,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs. When Micron’s profit margins grow, it means higher costs for others.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"For comparison with Micron's latest quarter, Allen Harris cites Nvidia's revenue growth of 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and operating margin of roughly 66%; the item does not specify the period for Nvidia's figures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 81,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume pricing. It puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis found that memory's share of the Vera Rubin (VR200) rack bill of materials rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the prior GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million standard estimate, indicating procurement structure can materially move final pricing.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 82,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\",\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that in 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures, and that next year (2027) Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capex.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 83,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com analyst Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — a demand driver for Nvidia GPUs and memory chips.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in reporting its latest quarter, announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote that demand for NAND and DRAM has been strong from the data center build-out while supply hasn't kept up, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed, that new capacity for many industry players won't be online until 2027 or later — keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half — and that this could push memory prices even higher.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 84,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation — nearly double — an increase the analysis attributes to memory rather than the GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with the GPUs not being the driver of the rack cost increase.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was largely absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that, as a share of the Vera Rubin VR200 bill of materials, memory rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the prior GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from roughly $7.8 million, showing that procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 85,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and states that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (2027). The 2027 figure is presented as Nvidia's claim, not a disclosed plan by the hyperscalers.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 86,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 and grow to roughly $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and related infrastructure, as cited by the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Per the article's comparison, Nvidia's latest reported quarter showed revenue growth of about 85% year over year, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66% — below Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin and 80.4% operating margin in Micron's fiscal Q3 2026 — though the article says Nvidia still earns more dollars.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron's latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia's 85 percent. Micron's GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia's 74.9 percent. Micron's operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia's roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs; the article adds that a memory shortage could raise consumer-electronics prices and that rising memory-company margins mean higher costs for device makers, server makers and cloud providers. The connection to Nvidia (memory as an input cost to AI accelerators) is inferred, not stated in the item.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Unfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners. It could also raise costs for everyone else. A shortage of memory might affect the prices of consumer electronics.\",\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs. When Micron's profit margins grow, it means higher costs for others. If memory companies earn more, then device makers, server makers, cloud providers, and eventually consumers could end up paying more for electronics.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 87,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"In a Micron-versus-Nvidia comparison, Allen Harris (The Berkshire Edge) reported that Nvidia's latest quarterly revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin 74.9%, and its operating margin roughly 66%, adding that Nvidia still earns more dollars than Micron despite Micron's higher margins.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion (up from $9.30 billion a year earlier, roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year ago, a gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, which as a memory supplier reflects surging AI memory demand and pricing power.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026, grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 88,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported, for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by Berkshire Edge columnist Allen Harris.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Berkshire Edge columnist Allen Harris reported that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin 74.9% and its operating margin roughly 66%, and that while Nvidia still earns more dollars, Micron grew faster and showed fatter margins in the latest quarter (Micron 346% growth, 84.6% GAAP gross margin, 80.4% operating margin).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars. Micron is growing faster right now and, at least in the latest quarter, is showing even fatter margins.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, according to Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a figure that includes chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 89,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027) — the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend on which Nvidia's GPU demand depends.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 90,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra — reach first shipments (the step that brings revenue) by the end of calendar 2027, rather than slipping to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 rack-scale systems (and the larger NVL576 optical system) actually begin shipping in calendar 2027, or are delayed to 2028 or later.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2027 — the debut year the item says Kyber had been slated for; its outcome can be checked against product-availability and shipment disclosures by then.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips, slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules inside the system remains difficult to manufacture. This is a named research firm's report, not a confirmed product schedule; Nvidia rejected it.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" asserting that its product schedule, including the planned 2027 debut of the Kyber rack architecture, still stands.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-1\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said a backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together to deliver similar computing power was cancelled after heavy pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers, who found the design odd and operationally burdensome.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a larger Nvidia system that links eight racks via optical connections — is also likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the Kyber delays and the cancelled two-rack workaround leave Nvidia with no proven way to expand the scale-up size of its Rubin Ultra systems, predicting this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the AI compute market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the item.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-3.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{k: ([{x:v for x,v in z.items() if x in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"summary\\\",\\\"links\\\")} for z in e[k]] if k==\\\"kept_evidence\\\" else [{x:v for x,v in z.items() if x in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"question\\\")} for z in e[k]] if k in (\\\"open_theses\\\",\\\"new_theses\\\") else e[k]) for k in (\\\"n\\\",\\\"claims\\\",\\\"link_rule\\\",\\\"open_theses\\\",\\\"new_theses\\\",\\\"answer_usable\\\",\\\"kept_evidence\\\")} for e in p],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-3.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{k: ([{x:v for x,v in z.items() if x in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"summary\\\",\\\"links\\\")} for z in e[k]] if k==\\\"kept_evidence\\\" else [{x:v for x,v in z.items() if x in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"question\\\")} for z in e[k]] if k in (\\\"open_theses\\\",\\\"new_theses\\\") else e[k]) for k in (\\\"n\\\",\\\"claims\\\",\\\"link_rule\\\",\\\"open_theses\\\",\\\"new_theses\\\",\\\"answer_usable\\\",\\\"kept_evidence\\\")} for e in p],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"n\": 61, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per Morgan Stanley's analysis.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev4\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from roughly $7.8 million, indicating procurement structure can move final per-rack pricing materially.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 62, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}], \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-kyber\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture (the NVL144 cabinet designed to house its Vera Rubin Ultra chips) begin shipping by the end of 2027, its originally slated debut, or slip to 2028?\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra chips and originally slated to debut in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules within the system remains difficult to manufacture. The report was published in a post on Monday [relative to the July 5-6, 2026 article].\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan — bolting two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power — was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack architecture was delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are due to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 63, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rises from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing procurement structure materially moves the total.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 64, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from their planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to the article's rendering of Nvidia's claim.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 65, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking. The estimate is cited by Allen Harris of The Berkshire Edge as the capital-spending backdrop supporting the AI rally; it is a broader total-AI-infrastructure measure and does not give a specific 2027 figure for the big four hyperscalers.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"In a comparison of Nvidia and Micron, The Berkshire Edge (Allen Harris) cites Nvidia's figures for its latest reported period: revenue growth of about 85 percent, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9 percent and an operating margin of roughly 66 percent, versus Micron's 346 percent revenue growth, 84.6 percent gross margin and 80.4 percent operating margin. The item notes Nvidia still earns more dollars. No reporting period is stated for Nvidia's figures.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, a supplier of memory/HBM used alongside AI accelerators, reported for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346 percent growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a gross margin of 84.6 percent (up from 37.7 percent) and an operating margin of 80.4 percent (up from 23.3 percent). The item attributes the boom partly to Nvidia's AI success spilling over into memory demand.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs — a concrete signal of memory/storage cost inflation that bears on input costs for memory-dependent hardware including AI accelerators.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 66, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, as reported by fool.com.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, as reported by fool.com.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 67, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, as reported by MarketScale.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis cited by MarketScale, NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and MarketScale's key-facts summary states that procurement structure can move final rack pricing by $1+ million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 68, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, i.e. the system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis shows memory's share of the Vera Rubin rack rose to 25-30% from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley breakdown reported by the article, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e7\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure materially moves the price.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 69, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's latest reported quarter produced revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, according to figures cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge comparing Nvidia with Micron. The item does not specify which fiscal quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, a supplier of AI memory including high-bandwidth memory used alongside AI accelerators, reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth); GAAP net income was $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin was 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin was 80.4% versus 23.3%, per Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The action is a pass-through of rising memory costs, a component input also relevant to AI hardware.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, according to Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 70, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank adds that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, moving final pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported by MarketScale.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase from the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory rises to 25-30% of the rack bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200 while the GPU's share falls from about 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}, {\"n\": 71, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers, which plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year), will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capex in 2027 (next year), as reported by fool.com on 7 July 2026.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 72, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"fool.com contributor Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, and that this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs. The article gives no company-level capex breakdown and attributes no source for the aggregates.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"fool.com contributor Keithen Drury wrote that memory (NAND and DRAM) supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as a result, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices higher still. DRAM is described as the high-speed memory NVIDIA's GPUs use.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in reporting its most recent quarter, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to fool.com's Keithen Drury.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which fool.com's Keithen Drury said is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate; the article states no figure for NVIDIA's expected revenue.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 73, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion the group plans to spend this year (2026), according to the fool.com article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 74, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The article reports NVIDIA's latest reported-quarter financials alongside Micron's: year-on-year revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that NVIDIA still earns more absolute dollars. The article does not specify which fiscal quarter these figures cover.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported third-fiscal-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; gross margin of 84.6%, up from 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4%, up from 23.3%. The article attributes the surge to AI-driven memory demand spilling over from NVIDIA's success.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\", \"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 75, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"The article reports NVIDIA's latest-quarter financial results in a comparison with Micron: NVIDIA's revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin was 74.9%, and its operating margin was roughly 66%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other plumbing.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}]}, {\"n\": 76, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"In an article dated 7 July 2026, fool.com author Keithen Drury projects that the AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures will be $650 billion in 2026 and could exceed $1 trillion in 2027, adding that this will drive even higher demand for Nvidia GPUs (and memory chips).\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said (reported as recent news) that tight market conditions could last until 2028. The article notes memory supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, memory chip prices have skyrocketed, and new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least another year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher. Nvidia's GPUs use DRAM, so these memory-market conditions bear on Nvidia's input costs and supply.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided next-quarter revenue to about $50 billion, which the article says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying roughly twice that figure in expected Nvidia quarterly revenue. As a memory supplier, Micron's revenue guidance reflects strong memory demand and pricing, an input-cost signal for Nvidia's GPUs.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 77, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}], \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-1\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra generation — begin shipping on its original 2027 schedule, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis claims?\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because its multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-1\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to obtain similar scale-up capacity for Rubin Ultra — has been scrapped, cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back over its odd design and heavy operational burden.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 will run 20% above Wall Street consensus.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 78, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}], \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-kyber\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra — begin shipping on its original 2027 timetable, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e-kyber-delay\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post on Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, the server cabinet slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules inside the system remains difficult to manufacture. CNBC framed the report as one of a string of reported setbacks raising questions about NVIDIA's product roadmap.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-nvl576-delay\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, NVIDIA's larger rack system that links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-backup-cancelled\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's operational burden; the firm said this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra and predicted it could give AMD and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-nvda-denial\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that the Kyber rack architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-rubin-shipping\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-dc-revenue-estimate\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 79, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-10\", \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-memory\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said (announced 'recently') that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, following a quarter it described with huge growth and strong forward expectations. The item reports memory chip prices have skyrocketed on the demand/supply imbalance and that new industry memory capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-capex\", \"summary\": \"The article projects that AI hyperscalers plan to spend about $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, which the author says would drive higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 80, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev4\", \"summary\": \"For comparison with Micron's latest quarter, Allen Harris cites Nvidia's revenue growth of 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and operating margin of roughly 66%; the item does not specify the period for Nvidia's figures.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 81, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume pricing. It puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis found that memory's share of the Vera Rubin (VR200) rack bill of materials rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the prior GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million standard estimate, indicating procurement structure can materially move final pricing.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 82, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The article states that in 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures, and that next year (2027) Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capex.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}, {\"n\": 83, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-10\", \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Fool.com analyst Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — a demand driver for Nvidia GPUs and memory chips.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in reporting its latest quarter, announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote that demand for NAND and DRAM has been strong from the data center build-out while supply hasn't kept up, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed, that new capacity for many industry players won't be online until 2027 or later — keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half — and that this could push memory prices even higher.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}]}, {\"n\": 84, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation — nearly double — an increase the analysis attributes to memory rather than the GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with the GPUs not being the driver of the rack cost increase.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was largely absent from earlier systems.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that, as a share of the Vera Rubin VR200 bill of materials, memory rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the prior GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from roughly $7.8 million, showing that procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e7\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}]}, {\"n\": 85, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026, and states that Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (2027). The 2027 figure is presented as Nvidia's claim, not a disclosed plan by the hyperscalers.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 86, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 and grow to roughly $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and related infrastructure, as cited by the article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Per the article's comparison, Nvidia's latest reported quarter showed revenue growth of about 85% year over year, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66% — below Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin and 80.4% operating margin in Micron's fiscal Q3 2026 — though the article says Nvidia still earns more dollars.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs; the article adds that a memory shortage could raise consumer-electronics prices and that rising memory-company margins mean higher costs for device makers, server makers and cloud providers. The connection to Nvidia (memory as an input cost to AI accelerators) is inferred, not stated in the item.\", \"links\": []}]}, {\"n\": 87, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"In a Micron-versus-Nvidia comparison, Allen Harris (The Berkshire Edge) reported that Nvidia's latest quarterly revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin 74.9%, and its operating margin roughly 66%, adding that Nvidia still earns more dollars than Micron despite Micron's higher margins.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion (up from $9.30 billion a year earlier, roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year ago, a gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, which as a memory supplier reflects surging AI memory demand and pricing power.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026, grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 88, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported, for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by Berkshire Edge columnist Allen Harris.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Berkshire Edge columnist Allen Harris reported that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin 74.9% and its operating margin roughly 66%, and that while Nvidia still earns more dollars, Micron grew faster and showed fatter margins in the latest quarter (Micron 346% growth, 84.6% GAAP gross margin, 80.4% operating margin).\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, according to Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a figure that includes chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 89, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-8\", \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-9\", \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"}], \"new_theses\": [], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027) — the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend on which Nvidia's GPU demand depends.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}, {\"n\": 90, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"open_theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}], \"new_theses\": [{\"id\": \"new-kyber-1\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra — reach first shipments (the step that brings revenue) by the end of calendar 2027, rather than slipping to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"answer_usable\": true, \"kept_evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips, slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules inside the system remains difficult to manufacture. This is a named research firm's report, not a confirmed product schedule; Nvidia rejected it.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" asserting that its product schedule, including the planned 2027 debut of the Kyber rack architecture, still stands.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-1\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said a backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together to deliver similar computing power was cancelled after heavy pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers, who found the design odd and operationally burdensome.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a larger Nvidia system that links eight racks via optical connections — is also likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the Kyber delays and the cancelled two-rack workaround leave Nvidia with no proven way to expand the scale-up size of its Rubin Ultra systems, predicting this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the AI compute market.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-6\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the item.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-3.json\\\"));\nfor e in p[6:24]:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",e[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"usable\\\",e[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"claims\\\",[x[\\\"claim_id\\\"] for x in e[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\",e[\\\"link_rule\\\"]); print(\\\"THESES\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+e[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in x[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capex\\\",\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"kyber\\\"])]); print(\\\"REF\\\",[(x[\\\"stored_id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]]); print(\\\"EVIDENCE\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"],x[\\\"links\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-3.json\\\"));\nfor e in p[6:24]:\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",e[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"usable\\\",e[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"claims\\\",[x[\\\"claim_id\\\"] for x in e[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\",e[\\\"link_rule\\\"]); print(\\\"THESES\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"question\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+e[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in x[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capex\\\",\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"kyber\\\"])]); print(\\\"REF\\\",[(x[\\\"stored_id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]]); print(\\\"EVIDENCE\\\",[(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"summary\\\"],x[\\\"links\\\"]) for x in e[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n 67 usable True claims ['M1', 'M2', 'M3'] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('E2', \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"), ('E5', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.'), ('E4', 'The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.')]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, as reported by MarketScale.\", ['clean-4']), ('e2', \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", []), ('e3', \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis cited by MarketScale, NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", ['clean-2']), ('e4', 'MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', ['clean-2']), ('e5', \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and MarketScale's key-facts summary states that procurement structure can move final rack pricing by $1+ million per rack.\", ['clean-4'])]\n\n 68 usable True claims ['M1', 'M2', 'M3'] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('E2', \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"), ('E5', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.'), ('E4', 'The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.')]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, i.e. the system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\", []), ('e2', 'Morgan Stanley estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.', []), ('e3', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.', []), ('e4', \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis shows memory's share of the Vera Rubin rack rose to 25-30% from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", []), ('e5', \"According to the Morgan Stanley breakdown reported by the article, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", ['clean-2']), ('e6', 'The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', []), ('e7', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure materially moves the price.', [])]\n\n 69 usable True claims ['G'] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nREF [('ev-4', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.')]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Nvidia's latest reported quarter produced revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, according to figures cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge comparing Nvidia with Micron. The item does not specify which fiscal quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\", []), ('e2', 'Micron Technology, a supplier of AI memory including high-bandwidth memory used alongside AI accelerators, reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth); GAAP net income was $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin was 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin was 80.4% versus 23.3%, per Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', []), ('e3', 'Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The action is a pass-through of rising memory costs, a component input also relevant to AI hardware.', []), ('e4', 'Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, according to Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 70 usable True claims ['M1'] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nREF [('ev2', \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank adds that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, moving final pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\", ['clean-3']), ('e2', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported by MarketScale.\", ['clean-3']), ('e3', \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase from the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory rises to 25-30% of the rack bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200 while the GPU's share falls from about 63% to 51%.\", ['clean-3']), ('e4', 'MarketScale reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', ['clean-3'])]\n\n 71 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nREF [('e1', \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('ev-1', 'Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers, which plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year), will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capex in 2027 (next year), as reported by fool.com on 7 July 2026.', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 72 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('e1', \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', 'fool.com contributor Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, and that this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs. The article gives no company-level capex breakdown and attributes no source for the aggregates.', ['clean-4']), ('e2', \"fool.com contributor Keithen Drury wrote that memory (NAND and DRAM) supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as a result, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices higher still. DRAM is described as the high-speed memory NVIDIA's GPUs use.\", []), ('e3', \"Micron Technology, in reporting its most recent quarter, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to fool.com's Keithen Drury.\", []), ('e4', \"Micron Technology guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which fool.com's Keithen Drury said is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate; the article states no figure for NVIDIA's expected revenue.\", [])]\n\n 73 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev-1', 'Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.')]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', 'Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion the group plans to spend this year (2026), according to the fool.com article.', ['clean-4'])]\n\n 74 usable True claims ['G'] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev-goldman-capex', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.')]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"The article reports NVIDIA's latest reported-quarter financials alongside Micron's: year-on-year revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that NVIDIA still earns more absolute dollars. The article does not specify which fiscal quarter these figures cover.\", []), ('e2', \"Micron reported third-fiscal-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; gross margin of 84.6%, up from 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4%, up from 23.3%. The article attributes the surge to AI-driven memory demand spilling over from NVIDIA's success.\", ['clean-4', 'clean-6']), ('e3', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.', ['clean-4'])]\n\n 75 usable True claims ['G'] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev-4', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.')]\nEVIDENCE [('ev-1', \"The article reports NVIDIA's latest-quarter financial results in a comparison with Micron: NVIDIA's revenue growth was about 85%, its GAAP gross margin was 74.9%, and its operating margin was roughly 66%.\", []), ('ev-2', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other plumbing.', ['clean-4']), ('ev-3', 'Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.', ['clean-5']), ('ev-4', 'Micron reported for its third fiscal quarter of 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.', ['clean-5'])]\n\n 76 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nREF [('e1', \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('ev-1', \"In an article dated 7 July 2026, fool.com author Keithen Drury projects that the AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures will be $650 billion in 2026 and could exceed $1 trillion in 2027, adding that this will drive even higher demand for Nvidia GPUs (and memory chips).\", ['clean-6']), ('ev-2', \"Micron Technology said (reported as recent news) that tight market conditions could last until 2028. The article notes memory supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, memory chip prices have skyrocketed, and new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least another year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher. Nvidia's GPUs use DRAM, so these memory-market conditions bear on Nvidia's input costs and supply.\", []), ('ev-3', \"Micron Technology guided next-quarter revenue to about $50 billion, which the article says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying roughly twice that figure in expected Nvidia quarterly revenue. As a memory supplier, Micron's revenue guidance reflects strong memory demand and pricing, an input-cost signal for Nvidia's GPUs.\", [])]\n\n 77 usable True claims ['K1', 'K2'] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('new-1', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Vera Rubin Ultra generation — begin shipping on its original 2027 schedule, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis claims?\")]\nREF [('E1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\"), ('E3', \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because its multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", ['new-1', 'clean-3']), ('e2', 'Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis\\'s report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \"Our roadmap is intact.\"', ['new-1', 'clean-3']), ('e3', \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\", ['clean-3']), ('e4', \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to obtain similar scale-up capacity for Rubin Ultra — has been scrapped, cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back over its odd design and heavy operational burden.\", ['new-1']), ('e5', \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 will run 20% above Wall Street consensus.\", [])]\n\n 78 usable True claims ['K1', 'K2'] rule Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('new-kyber', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra — begin shipping on its original 2027 timetable, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev-1', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"), ('ev-2', 'NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis\\'s report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \"Our roadmap is intact.\"')]\nEVIDENCE [('e-kyber-delay', \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post on Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, the server cabinet slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules inside the system remains difficult to manufacture. CNBC framed the report as one of a string of reported setbacks raising questions about NVIDIA's product roadmap.\", ['new-kyber', 'clean-2']), ('e-nvl576-delay', \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576, NVIDIA's larger rack system that links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", ['new-kyber']), ('e-backup-cancelled', \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's operational burden; the firm said this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra and predicted it could give AMD and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\", ['new-kyber']), ('e-nvda-denial', 'NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis\\'s report that the Kyber rack architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \"Our roadmap is intact.\"', ['new-kyber', 'clean-2']), ('e-rubin-shipping', \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\", ['clean-2']), ('e-dc-revenue-estimate', \"SemiAnalysis projected that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", [])]\n\n 79 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev3', 'The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.')]\nEVIDENCE [('ev-memory', \"Micron Technology said (announced 'recently') that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, following a quarter it described with huge growth and strong forward expectations. The item reports memory chip prices have skyrocketed on the demand/supply imbalance and that new industry memory capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\", ['clean-5']), ('ev-capex', 'The article projects that AI hyperscalers plan to spend about $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, which the author says would drive higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.', ['clean-4'])]\n\n 80 usable True claims ['G'] rule The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev-4', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.')]\nEVIDENCE [('ev1', 'Micron Technology reported revenue of $41.46 billion for its third fiscal quarter of 2026, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', ['clean-5']), ('ev2', 'Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', ['clean-4']), ('ev3', 'Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as reported by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge.', ['clean-5']), ('ev4', \"For comparison with Micron's latest quarter, Allen Harris cites Nvidia's revenue growth of 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and operating margin of roughly 66%; the item does not specify the period for Nvidia's figures.\", [])]\n\n 81 usable True claims ['M1'] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nREF [('ev2', \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume pricing. It puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage.\", ['clean-6']), ('e2', \"The article reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", ['clean-3']), ('e3', \"Morgan Stanley's analysis found that memory's share of the Vera Rubin (VR200) rack bill of materials rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the prior GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", ['clean-6']), ('e4', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million standard estimate, indicating procurement structure can materially move final pricing.', ['clean-6']), ('e5', 'The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 82 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-6', \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"), ('clean-7', \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\")]\nREF [('e1', \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\")]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', 'The article states that in 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures, and that next year (2027) Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capex.', ['clean-6'])]\n\n 83 usable True claims ['C26', 'C27'] rule Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?'), ('clean-6', \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('ev3', 'The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.')]\nEVIDENCE [('ev1', 'Fool.com analyst Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — a demand driver for Nvidia GPUs and memory chips.', ['clean-4']), ('ev2', 'Micron Technology, in reporting its latest quarter, announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.', ['clean-5']), ('ev3', \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote that demand for NAND and DRAM has been strong from the data center build-out while supply hasn't kept up, that memory chip prices have skyrocketed, that new capacity for many industry players won't be online until 2027 or later — keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half — and that this could push memory prices even higher.\", ['clean-5'])]\n\n 84 usable True claims ['M1', 'M2', 'M3'] rule Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\nTHESES [('clean-4', 'How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?'), ('clean-5', \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\")]\nREF [('E2', \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"), ('E5', 'Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.'), ('E4', 'The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.')]\nEVIDENCE [('e1', \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation — nearly double — an increase the analysis attributes to memory rather than the GPUs.\", ['clean-4']), ('e2', 'Morgan Stanley estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with the GPUs not being the driver of the rack cost increase.', ['clean-4']), ('e3', 'Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was largely absent from earlier systems.', ['clean-4']), ('e4', \"Morgan Stanley estimates that, as a share of the Vera Rubin VR200 bill of materials, memory rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the prior GB200 generation, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\", ['clean-4']), ('e5', \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", ['clean-2']), ('e6', \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from roughly $7.8 million, showing that procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\", ['clean-4']), ('e7', 'The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.', ['clean-2'])]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"n\":61,\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"M1\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"ev2\",\"why\":\"The item states Morgan Stanley's $2 million estimate and 435% increase and links it to a thesis.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M2\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"ev5\",\"why\":\"The item states the $6.7 million self-sourcing estimate and links it to theses.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M3\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"ev4\",\"why\":\"The item describes the supply constraints and higher DDR5 prices and links them to a thesis.\"}]}\n{\"n\":62,\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"K1\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"e1\",\"why\":\"The reported delay is linked to the new Kyber timing thesis.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"K2\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"e4\",\"why\":\"NVIDIA's rejection is linked to the new Kyber timing thesis.\"}]}\n{\"n\":63,\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"M1\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":false,\"item\":\"e2\",\"why\":\"The estimate and increase are kept but the item has no thesis link.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M2\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":false,\"item\":\"e5\",\"why\":\"The self-sourcing estimate is kept but the item has no thesis link.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M3\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":false,\"item\":\"e4\",\"why\":\"The supply constraints and DDR5 price increase are kept but the item has no thesis link.\"}]}\n{\"n\":64,\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"C26\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"ev-1\",\"why\":\"The item states the planned $650 billion and links it to the open capex thesis.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"C27\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"ev-1\",\"why\":\"The item states NVIDIA's projection above $1 trillion and links it to the 2027 capex thesis.\"}]}\n{\"n\":65,\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"G\",\"kept\":true,\"linked_as_required\":true,\"item\":\"e1\",